<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kondavidu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kondavidu is a hill fort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the 14th century it became the capital of the Reddi Kingdom, whose kings ruled coastal Andhra from it until Vijayanagara forces took it around 1424. It later changed hands between the Gajapatis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, Vijayanagara under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a>, who captured it in 1515, and the Golconda Sultanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kondavidu.jpg" alt="Kondavidu" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R., William (fl. 1784-1816) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kondavid-drug._Signed_%27W.R.%27.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The forts stand on a high ridge of a small range of hills about 16 miles west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a>, at an average height of 1,500 feet and 1,700 feet at the highest point. A steep but short path climbs from the north; the usual way up is a gentler trek of about 2 miles. The kingdom ruled from here lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> to the north and the Gundlakamma River to the south. Water came from three ponds, the Mutyalama, Puttalamma and Vedulla Cheruvu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kondavidu is said to have been founded in 1115 as a fortified township by Gopanna, a commander of Buddhavarma of the Telugu Chola dynasty, and later came under the Kakatiyas. Remains of a Buddhist stupa of the later Satavahana period, the 1st to 2nd century CE, found in 2019 under a ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple, show the site is older still. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> and Andhra in 1323, the Reddis joined the confederation movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas">Musunuri Nayaks</a> that drove them out. Prolaya Vema Reddy, who ruled until 1353, built forts to defend his kingdom, Kondavidu among them, and moved his capital there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addanki">Addanki</a>.</p><p>After the Reddis the fort passed through many hands, among them the Bahmanis, the Gajapatis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, who fought for it several times in the 16th century, captured it in 1579 and renamed it Murtazanagar. The army of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> came in 1687. The French took it in 1752 and fortified it heavily, and the British East India Company gained control in 1788 but abandoned it early in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The three forts on the ridge are in ruins. The main fort, built by the Reddis at nearly 320 metres, keeps granite ramparts, magazines, granaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, a three-storeyed gate and a building with inscriptions 110 metres long, and a single wall of 30 kilometres runs along the hills. At the foot of the hill stands the Gopinathaswami temple, and a mosque stands within the fort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kondaveedu Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest commander was Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529), who defeated the Gajapati rulers of Odisha in campaigns between 1513 and 1519, taking Udayagiri and <b>Kondavidu</b>, and in 1520 defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the Battle of Raichur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He made Addanki and then <b>Kondavidu</b> his capital, and the dynasty extended along the coast from the Krishna to the Godavari.</blockquote><a href="/state/reddi-kingdom">Reddi Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
